WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.306 --> 00:00:22.189
You can't afford to not be good at this.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:29.110
You're dependent on your job as a leader
to get other people to take actions.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.810
You need to be skilled in relationships,
in influence and communication.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.730
There's no getting around it.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.830
So it gets labeled as soft skills but
it's really a hard business driver.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:48.600
And there's hard science underneath
it that we understand how it works.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.030
So it's not just soft.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:55.220
It's a critical piece of being
an effective communicator.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.590
People talk about executive presence or
having a presence.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:04.350
And most people think it's something that
you're born with or that person just has.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.037
It turns out, it's just a set of behaviors
if presence is something that you do.

00:01:09.037 --> 00:01:13.625
[MUSIC]

00:01:13.625 --> 00:01:18.120
There's a set of behaviors that I like to
encapsulate down to about five points on

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.850
either side of that equation.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.040
On the authoritative
side there is slowness,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.540
in how you move and how you speak.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:33.050
Stillness, just being very
still like right now.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.560
I'm not moving my head very much at all.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.550
That's a choice, normally I have
a little bit more motion in me.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.920
So, stillness.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.863
Silence Is very powerful, and

00:01:46.863 --> 00:01:51.780
if you can be comfortable in silence, then
you can use that to be more authoritative.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.830
Symmetry has to do with
do I slant a little, or

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.520
does my head go off to the side.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.360
I would say this is a little less
authoritative, than what this is.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.160
And then space.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.590
What space do you own?

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.620
And can I reach out and
touch someone's shoulder or

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:13.660
shake their hand and make that
first move to invade their space.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:15.530
Those are all on the authoritative side.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:20.117
[MUSIC]

00:02:20.117 --> 00:02:24.470
So on the other side with approachable,
there are five Fs.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.040
And that is filling space.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.730
If you can't let a silence happen,
that's more approachable.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:34.830
If you speak or move fast,

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:38.020
you're usually trying to get all your
words in before you get interrupted.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.270
That's not a place of power.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:44.170
You know that you don't
have the control to talk so

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:47.010
you try to get everything
in all in one space.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.769
The next one is folded.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:54.111
And that's where you're folding your body,
like crossing your arms,

00:02:54.111 --> 00:02:59.250
crossing your legs is all a way of sort
of protecting yourself and closing in.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.910
It's a little more approachable.

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.790
And even just that, sort of,
twisting on one foot,

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:08.030
or crossing one leg over the other,
is a little closer together.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:12.360
And it's related to fidgeting, so if you
do that and the motion keeps happening,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.310
that also gets very close
to what flirting is.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:22.530
And there's a lot of little gestures and
motions that we do that are flirting,

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:27.860
including making and
breaking eye contact is more flirtatious.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:31.070
And the last one is about giving space.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.560
You allow people into your space.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.840
You invite people to take your space.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.945
So those are all of the characteristics
of being on the approachable side.

00:03:42.945 --> 00:03:47.683
[MUSIC]

00:03:47.683 --> 00:03:51.175
The first thing is awareness and you
have that if you're asking the question.

00:03:51.175 --> 00:03:56.020
And the second thing is to just look
at yourself, just start to observe.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:59.610
How do I act in different situations and
how do other people act?

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:05.257
Often I talk to students and they want
some coaching because they're ready for

00:04:05.257 --> 00:04:05.860
a promotion.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:10.530
They want to move up into that partner
position or into the next level.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.850
And I always tell them to observe and

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:18.920
report on how do those people act behave,
verbally, non verbally.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.875
Because you need to act
in that same way and

00:04:21.875 --> 00:04:26.856
what got you to where you are isn't
what will get you to the next stage,

00:04:26.856 --> 00:04:30.582
but you have a perfect
opportunity to just observe.

00:04:30.582 --> 00:04:35.139
So that's the first place and
the second place is to try and

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:38.869
make some change,
any change and practice it.

00:04:38.869 --> 00:04:43.586
[MUSIC]

00:04:43.586 --> 00:04:48.096
Improvisational acting or
any acting class from community

00:04:48.096 --> 00:04:53.360
theater to a professional school hones and
works on these tools.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:59.540
They're also really fun to do,
it's a fun place to play and

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:02.680
many people are afraid of going to do it.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.470
And those classes are some of
the safest places in the world.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:10.850
Because to do that work you have to create
a really safe space for people to take

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:15.170
some risks and try to show up in
different ways than they normally are.

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:19.560
So their people are very skilled and the
groups that are there are very supportive.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.090
So in most cases people
come in a little afraid and

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.025
leave just elated because it's so
much fun.

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:30.781
[MUSIC]

00:05:30.781 --> 00:05:33.420
It starts with the fact that
acting is not pretending.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.078
Most people think it is.

00:05:36.078 --> 00:05:39.970
And if you see actors that
are not great in film and

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.670
TV, they are the ones that are pretending.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:46.306
Acting is about finding a truthful place.

00:05:46.306 --> 00:05:48.590
So when you see a really good actor,

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:54.040
you're seeing how would that person
actually show up in that setting.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:59.260
So if it's a dangerous setting and
scary things are happening,

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:05.260
you're seeing that person react in
the way they show up as a scared person.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:11.080
And when we see that, it's really
believable, because it's authentic.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:17.070
Authentic means in tune with the emotional
state that you're actually in,

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.490
and not something separate.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:24.961
So an example of that would be
if I try to put on mad for you.

00:06:24.961 --> 00:06:27.770
I think this doesn't really look mad.

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:29.860
It looks kind of cartoonish.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:34.650
It's like when you ask a small
child to show me your mad face.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.500
It's not really mad.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.349
It's a mask that they're putting on.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.700
When you're really authentically mad,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.970
you show up in a way that it's clear
that you're communicating that emotion.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:47.540
That's what authentic is.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:52.350
Everyone can be authentically
authoritative or

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:55.490
authentically approachable,
anywhere along this range.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:58.630
They will do it slightly differently.

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:04.290
My authentic authority, my authentic
powerful is different than yours,

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.510
which is different than everyone
else that is out there.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:12.130
But it's truthful, and if it's truthful,
then we believe it and

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.830
it's really part of you.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:17.380
So acting is not about putting on
the mask or the costume or the character,

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.434
it's about finding the truthful place and
bringing that out.

00:07:20.434 --> 00:07:25.207
[MUSIC]

00:07:25.207 --> 00:07:30.300
Before you walk into the room, you could
put on these gestures and these poses.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.530
You can do it by yourself,
you can go to the restroom and do it.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.840
You can do it in the elevator on
the way up or around the corner.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:42.190
Expanding your body, saying your speech or
a few lines to yourself

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:46.660
using the slow and low tones if
that's how you want to show up.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:51.670
And by doing that preparation,
it changes you inside and

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:54.860
it will carry over into the meeting and
that sets you up for success.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:00.430
You can start that before
you leave the house.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:07.490
So I have a couple of playlists that
are ultimate, epic movie soundtracks that

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:13.550
are the important themes from superhero
movies, and I start listening to that.

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:16.530
And I know what's going on.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:21.610
I know I am tricking myself and
it still works every time.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:25.090
And I laugh at myself noticing
that it's working and

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:26.470
then I really feel the emotions.

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:27.650
And so

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:32.670
today I had a lecture before I came in on
the car, I was listening to that music.

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:36.859
And behind the scenes backstage
before I stepped onto the stage,

00:08:36.859 --> 00:08:39.310
I had the music on and I raised my arms.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.903
I'm in the back in the green room,
nobody's around and

00:08:42.903 --> 00:08:45.049
even if they are, I don't care.

00:08:45.049 --> 00:08:48.993
But using all those techniques
to get my body ready,

00:08:48.993 --> 00:08:52.610
to think about how good it's going to go.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:57.360
And then, I walk on stage very confident,
full of the way I want to show up.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.922
[MUSIC]

00:09:01.922 --> 00:09:05.794
Once you know, if you are aware enough
to notice it's going on in you,

00:09:05.794 --> 00:09:08.820
you are aware enough
to then make a change.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:13.030
And if it's going poorly, almost any
change you make will make it better.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:18.340
If you notice that you're babbling
a little and you're going on too much or

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:22.950
talking too fast,
as soon as you do, stop and

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:27.010
take a pause, take a breath,
get a drink of water.

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:34.110
Even say, just a second, I want to check
my notes on this before I continue.

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:40.710
And make them wait, and it gives you a
chance to breath and to stop and to reset.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:42.160
So you can do that at any point,

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:48.610
from boardrooms to audiences in theaters,
they all are the same.

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:50.570
There's a piece of them
that will allow for

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:52.800
you to be a human being and
to show up in that way.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:59.366
And even in a aggressive
negotiation meeting with someone,

00:09:59.366 --> 00:10:05.305
they'll allow for you to do show up and
stop and pause and recover.

00:10:05.305 --> 00:10:07.920
It will not stop the meeting.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:11.797
It feels that way sometimes, but
I think you can always stop and reset.

00:10:11.797 --> 00:10:16.560
[MUSIC]

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.098
If I just start smiling a little more,
you're going to start smiling,

00:10:20.098 --> 00:10:23.130
and we're going to start
mirroring back and forth.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:27.430
And if I stop doing it,
it will pull you a little that way.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:34.930
You can watch in any room
the wave of crossed legs and

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:39.180
uncrossed legs happen as we
all get in sync in resonance.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:44.180
So we are always reacting to
each other and the reality is

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:49.840
we're all improvising our days,
that's why it's also a really useful tool.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:50.940
Because nobody gets up and

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:54.650
gets a script, well actors do, but
nobody gets a script for their day.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:58.980
We are all making it up all
the time we are all reacting to

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:00.940
everything that's going on.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:06.680
I think what we're talking about in the
work that I do is being more aware of it,

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.530
being more comfortable
to make more choices.

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:13.680
Reading the room, and
making choices about how we want to react.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:14.840
When it's important,

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:18.153
when it's meaningful and when we
want to do it in service of others.

00:11:18.153 --> 00:11:22.679
[MUSIC]

